  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSurface high pressure is in place following last night's cold\nfront. This will maintain dry and seasonable conditions through\nFriday. Water vapor satellite imagery shows moisture flowing\neastward into the region to allow for clouds to increase in the\nmid levels tonight. Starting Friday, ensemble guidance all \npoints toward upper ridging building into the Northern Plains\nwith a southwest flow pattern. This will trend temperatures \ntoward above average, particularly by Saturday. The way this \nupper ridging builds with moisture transport can also promote \nlow stratus, fog/drizzle - especially heading into November.\nThis continues to lower confidence in exactly how warm \ntemperatures get. With at least partial sun, temperatures could\nsoar well into the 60s Sunday into Monday.\n\n...Increasing Rain Chances...\n\nGetting into early to mid next week, ensemble guidance is mostly\nin agreement on some extent of rain event as an upper shortwave\ntrough in the Southwest translates into the Central Plains. \nWhile the best forcing looks to remain south of our area, there\ncould be enough influence from this feature to bring the next \nround of light to moderate rain into the forecast, heavily \nfavoring the southern zones. A lack of cold air behind the\nsystem results in near zero chance for frozen precipitation.",
